#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110355 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110629 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c7eec3a-50e3-5550-9e1e-def64ca9642a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Supplementary Angles From a Description"}] :question/id #uuid "62cec20b-28d4-5f08-9201-2d32bb741f1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-15141" :question/problem "Find the measure of an angle that's supplementary to an angle of $(7x -63)^\\circ.$" :question/worked-solution "Two angles are supplementary if their measures add up to $180^\\circ.$ \n\nSo, the measure of an angle that's supplementary to an angle of $(7x -63)^\\circ$ must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}-(7x-63)^{∘} & =(180-7x+63)^{∘} \\\\  & =(180+63-7x)^{∘} \\\\  & =(243-7x)^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110356 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6e34d9-29a2-55ed-9d28-258cbfd91600" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Quantity Using Supplementary Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "39e78adf-4714-5461-b3a4-c619019df41a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15142" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3fb947727e28a9c8424ea906ce8440f2795459278797242dd9e61c63f8ccd9c.png)\n\nSolve for $y$ given the figure above." :question/worked-solution "Since the two angles are supplementary, the sum of their measures is equal to $180^\\circ$. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y-{17}^{∘})+(y+{37}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4y+{20}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4y & ={160}^{∘} \\\\ y & ={40}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110357 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "931f7270-ec55-5c42-8c1b-6c7988f6c921" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Supplementary Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "4ebed10c-0a87-528a-ba18-67d960ba7692" :question/math-academy-id "e-15641" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a65c88ab4b81dcce668f9c936039b990cd4eb8b1d8576a6e53e2234eb16991c6.png)\n\nWhat angle is supplementary to $\\angle DGB$ in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/ea11964802b9ebca124a7f31b93fe58c30e619fc7279658bdcfcf7b683f24bb0.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to $180^\\circ,$ a straight angle.\n\nOnly $\\angle DGF$ is supplementary to $\\angle DGB$ in the given diagram."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110358 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ba5d861-c6bf-57df-aa00-7034aeb5e1ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Supplementary Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "ba553f7b-1750-5ffb-b6f8-81ad141368a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-15642" :question/problem "![](images/22/22e71949f4a63a7f650c38841bd67d3e5cb415a5c8819b0ea4ce80431bf6dbfb.png)\n\nGiven that $\\overline{AD}$ is a segment, what is the measure of $\\angle FOD?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/41/416aef1d024c1b926eb33bada7611bb1d345b0d9daa2d59730b05bab85617c05.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to $180^\\circ,$ a straight angle.\n\nSince $\\overline{AD}$ is a segment, we have that $\\angle AOF$ and $\\angle FOD$ are supplementary. \n\n$$\nm \\angle AOF + m \\angle FOD = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle FOD$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠FOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠AOF \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "931f7270-ec55-5c42-8c1b-6c7988f6c921" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Supplementary Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22fed3e3-8bd4-5726-86f1-6b5202e24ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠FGD" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498808} {:answer/id #uuid "244233c3-e76a-5ed5-8d96-d5dac0567d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠BGA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498811} {